# redis.conf.example — template for the Redis config file that holds `requirepass` (#448)
#
# Copy this to `redis.conf` NEXT TO THE COMPOSE FILE THAT USES IT, fill in the password, and set
# the ownership described below. `redis.conf` is gitignored; this example never contains a secret.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A FILE AND NOT `--requirepass` OR `environment:`
#
# Compose interpolates `${REDIS_PASSWORD}` at parse time, so with either of those forms the
# plaintext password is baked into the container's stored configuration and `docker inspect`
# prints it back. On the production host that is readable by the `deploy` user, and the
# self-hosted CI runner is that same host executing code from pull requests.
#
# Measured with a dummy value against a real redis:7-alpine container:
#
#     command: redis-server --requirepass ${REDIS_PASSWORD}   -> 3 hits in `docker inspect`
#     environment: REDIS_PASSWORD: ${REDIS_PASSWORD}          -> 1 hit  (.Config.Env)
#     command: redis-server /usr/local/etc/redis/redis.conf   -> 0 hits
#
# `environment:` is a lateral move, not a fix. Only a file the container reads at runtime keeps
# the value out of the container's configuration.
#
# Redis masks its own process title (`ps` shows `redis-server *:6379`, never the password), so
# Docker's stored config was the whole exposure for the server. The old healthcheck was worse
# than the server: `redis-cli -a <password> ping` ran as a real, argv-visible process every 10
# seconds for the life of the container. The replacement needs no credential at all — an
# unauthenticated PING against a protected server answers "NOAUTH Authentication required",
# which proves liveness just as well as PONG.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OWNERSHIP — GET THIS WRONG AND REDIS WILL NOT START
#
# The obvious instinct is "readable only by root". That crash-loops the container:
#
#     Fatal error, can't open config file '/usr/local/etc/redis/redis.conf': Permission denied
#
# The redis:7-alpine image drops to uid 999 (user `redis`) before reading its config. The file
# must be readable by that uid, not by root:
#
#     sudo chown 999:999 redis.conf
#     sudo chmod 400 redis.conf
#
# That is still root-or-redis only on the host; it is not world-readable.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APPLYING THIS TO THE SHARED CONTAINER
#
# The Redis that Muhabbet actually uses is `shared-redis`, which is defined OUTSIDE this
# repository (the multi-project stack under /opt/projects/infra). This repo cannot change how it
# starts. To apply the same fix there:
#
#   1. Create the conf file next to that stack's compose file, with the ownership above.
#   2. Change that service to:
#        command: ["redis-server", "/usr/local/etc/redis/redis.conf"]
#        volumes: [ "./redis.conf:/usr/local/etc/redis/redis.conf:ro", ... ]
#      and the credential-free healthcheck:
#        test: ["CMD-SHELL", "redis-cli ping 2>&1 | grep -qE 'PONG|NOAUTH'"]
#   3. ROTATE the password. The current one has been in `argv` for the life of the container and
#      is in whatever shell histories, logs and support bundles have touched this host. Put the
#      new value in the conf file AND in REDIS_PASSWORD in /opt/projects/Muhabbet/.env.prod.
#   4. Restart Redis, then restart the backend:
#        cd /opt/projects/Muhabbet
#        docker compose --env-file .env.prod -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d muhabbet-backend
#      `--env-file .env.prod` is mandatory; without it every secret resolves to empty and the JWT
#      boot guard crash-loops the container on a misleading "secret too short".
#   5. Confirm nothing leaked back:
#        docker inspect shared-redis | grep -c '<the new password>'   # must print 0
#
# Nothing of value is lost by rotating: presence keys, typing state, the WS fan-out channel and
# the OTP quota counters are all transient, and user sessions are JWTs that do not live in Redis.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The secret. Replace the placeholder — do not commit a filled-in copy of this file.
requirepass CHANGE_ME

# Everything below matches the tuning that used to be passed as command-line flags.
maxmemory 256mb
maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru
timeout 600
tcp-keepalive 300
databases 2
lazyfree-lazy-eviction yes
